CLAIMS=[
('In 30 years Nina has ~$952,000 net worth, a 401(k) yielding ~$38,000 vs $75,000 expenses, working to 66; Ian has ~$3,163,000 yielding $126,500, stopping at 51; a $2.2M gap.','30 years from now, Nina will have approximately','K02: 3,163,000 - 952,000 = 2,211,000 and 126,500/3,163,000 = 4.0% (reproduce). The model inputs are not disclosed. Internal inconsistency: "30 years from now" at age 32 is 62, yet the close says "At 55" for both, and Ian stops at 51 while compounding 30 years. At 35% of $60,000 ($21,000/yr) at 8% for 30 years the result is $2.38M; $3.16M needs higher returns or rising contributions. Illustrative composite.',[]),
('On a ~$83,000 median household income, saving 10% ($8,300) at 8% for 30 years grows to about $1,030,000; 4% of $1M is $41,000 vs average spending of $77,000, a $36,000 shortfall.','median US household income of about','CENSUSINC2: 2024 median household income $83,730 (verified). BLSCE2: average annual expenditures $77,158 (2023) / $78,535 (2024). K02: $8,300 at 8% for 30 years = $940,251 (end-of-year) or $1,015,471 (start-of-year), so $1.03M is slightly high; 4% x 1.03M = $41,200; 77,000 - 41,000 = 36,000. Mixes household-level spending with a single 10% saver and ignores Social Security.',['CENSUSINC2','BLSCE2']),
('The US personal savings rate right now is 4%.','The American personal savings rate right now','Not fetched; BEA personal saving rate was in the 4-5% range in early 2026 (analyst recall). Note the personal saving rate is a national-accounts aggregate, not the share of income each household saves for retirement. '+U,[]),
('A 50% savings rate reaches financial independence in about 17 years; 10% takes 51 years.','A 50%\nsavings rate reaches financial','Matches the widely cited Mr. Money Mustache table (5% real return, 4% withdrawal) (analyst recall, not fetched); the result depends on those assumptions.',[]),
('The average new car payment is $767/month; nearly one in five buyers takes an 84-month loan.','The average new car payment','Consistent with Edmunds 2025-2026 reporting (average new-vehicle payment in the mid-$700s; ~20% of new loans at 84 months) (analyst recall, not fetched). '+U,[]),
('Nina\'s $45,000 Honda loses ~20% on day one; by year five it is worth ~$18,000 while she owes $22,000.','Nina\'s $45,000 Honda loses','K02: 45,000 x 0.8 = 36,000 after day one. A 20% instant drop overstates typical first-day depreciation (around 10% is the common rule; ~20% over the first year, analyst recall). An $22,000 balance after five years implies a long (likely 84-month) loan. Illustrative.',[]),
('$700/month in an S&P 500 index fund at historical average returns for 30 years becomes about $1.58M.','$700 a month\ninvested in an S&P 500 index fund','K02: reproduces at 10% nominal compounded monthly ($1,582,342); at 8% it is $1,043,252. The 10% nominal assumption is not stated and conflicts with the 8% used elsewhere in the script.',[]),
('Across the 100 largest US metros buying costs 57% more per month than renting; Los Angeles 118%, San Jose 131%; home prices five times income, up from 3.5x.','Across the 100 largest US metros','RENTBUY: the 57% / 118% / 131% figures match a Home Abroad study of the 100 most populous cities as of March 2025, stale for an April 2026 upload; Realtor.com March 2026 (50 metros, starter homes) shows LA +80.7% and San Jose +74.0%. The price-to-income ratio is consistent with Harvard JCHS (analyst recall, not fetched).',['RENTBUY']),
('Shiller: real US home appreciation ~1%/year over 130 years vs ~7% real for the S&P 500.','Robert Shiller, who won a Nobel','Directionally correct: Shiller\'s long-run real home price index grows well under 1%/year on many spans; stocks ~6.5-7% real (analyst recall, not fetched). Comparison omits imputed rent and leverage, which are central to housing returns. '+U,[]),
('Property taxes average $4,800/year; maintenance 1-2% of a $380,000 home is $3,800-7,600; selling costs 8-10%.','property taxes averaging $4,800','K02: 1% and 2% of 380,000 = 3,800 and 7,600 (reproduce). The $4,800 average property tax is above ATTOM\'s ~$4,100-4,300 national average for single-family homes (analyst recall, not fetched). '+U,[]),
('A 1% advisory fee on a $500,000 portfolio growing at 7% over 30 years consumes about $934,000.','Greg\'s 1%\nfee consumes approximately','K02: 500,000 x (1.07^30 - 1.06^30) = $934,382 (reproduces, lump sum, no contributions).',[]),
('SPIVA: 94% of actively managed domestic equity funds underperform over 20 years; essentially none of the top-quartile funds stayed there four years later.','According to S&P\'s SPIVA Scorecard, 94%','SPIVA2025: secondary summaries of the SPIVA U.S. Year-End 2025 scorecard report ~95% of all domestic funds underperforming the S&P 1500 over 20 years (large-cap ~91%), so 94% is in range. Persistence is low but "essentially zero" is not confirmed.',['SPIVA2025']),
('Vanguard Total Stock Market Index charges 0.03%; active funds 1-1.5% plus a 1% advisory fee: 40-50 times more.','Vanguard\'s Total Stock Market\nIndex Fund charges 0.03%','VTI ETF 0.03%; VTSAX 0.04% (analyst recall). K02: 1.0/0.03 = 33x and 1.5/0.03 = 50x for fund fees alone, so "40-50 times" is approximately right only at the higher fund fee.',[]),
('MIT researchers found people spend 12-18% more with cards; McDonald\'s card transactions averaged $7 vs $4.50 cash.','MIT\nresearchers found that people spend 12','The MIT study (Prelec & Simester 2001) found much larger willingness-to-pay differences in an auction setting, not 12-18%; the 12-18% and McDonald\'s figures are popular uncited statistics (analyst recall, not fetched). '+U,[]),
('47% of cardholders carry a balance at ~21% APR on an average $6,500 balance.','For the 47% of\ncardholders who carry a balance','Not fetched; similar figures appear in Bankrate/LendingTree surveys. '+U,[]),
('Going against social norms activates the same neural pathways as physical pain.','going\nagainst social norms activates the same','Overstated: Klucharev et al. (2009) found conformity-related error signals in the rostral cingulate zone and ventral striatum; equating this with "physical pain" is an extrapolation (analyst recall, not fetched).',[]),
('Dutch researchers found neighbors of lottery winners increased car spending within a year.','Researchers in the Netherlands found','Consistent with Kuhn et al. (2011, American Economic Review) on the Dutch Postcode Lottery, where winners\' neighbours were more likely to buy cars (analyst recall, not fetched). Winners received cash and BMWs, not necessarily luxury cars.',[]),
('The median 401(k) balance for ages 55-64 is $95,642, generating ~$3,800/year vs $77,000 spending: a $73,000 gap.','The median 401k balance\nfor Americans age 55 to 64','VANGHAS: Vanguard How America Saves 2025 median for 55-64 was $95,642 (verified; Vanguard participants only, not all Americans; the 2026 edition reported $107,269 after publication). K02: 4% x 95,642 = $3,826; 77,000 - 3,826 = $73,174. The gap ignores Social Security and other assets.',['VANGHAS']),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "POV: You Stopped Following Normal Money Advice" uses the channel\'s POV second-person frame and a contrarian promise. The script compares two composite 32-year-olds (Nina, conventional; Ian, unconventional) across savings rate, car, house, advisor and credit cards. TYPICAL (1.00x) against peers that include two STRONG titles. Association only; no CTR or experiment evidence.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White field. Left: the recurring cartoon man in a white shirt covering his ears with a smug half-smile. Right: a black headline "I STOPPED LISTENING" and a curved arrow to a large pile of green cash beside a brown "FINANCE" book struck with a red X. The headline is first-person while the title is second-person POV. At 320x180 the headline, ears gesture and cash pile remain legible; the book label is unreadable.'
SCRIPT='A contrarian comparison: Tuesday-night banking-app hook, coworker retiring at 51, Nina vs Ian setup with 30-year outcomes, then line-by-line: 10% savings rate, savings rate vs years to FI, car payments and negative equity, $700/month invested, rent vs buy and Shiller housing returns, hidden home costs, 1% advisor fee and SPIVA, credit-card rewards and pain of paying, Ian\'s automated year, social-norm psychology and Dutch lottery neighbours, the playbook as a business model, a repeated outcome close and the median 401(k). Verified: median household income, CE spending, SPIVA 20-year (~95%), Vanguard $95,642 median, rent-vs-buy figures (but from March 2025). Reproduces: advisor fee $934K, $1.58M (at an unstated 10%), maintenance. Problems: Nina/Ian ages and horizons inconsistent (30 years vs "at 55" vs stopping at 51); $3.16M not reproducible from 35% of $60K at 8%; "$72,000 after tax" contradicts "around $60,000"; 20% day-one depreciation; MIT 12-18%; "physical pain" neuroscience. The stored ending is complete.'
